,"proxyFilter":null,"access":"public","size":-1,"subInfo":0,"appCategories":[],"industries":[],"languages":[],"largeThumbnail":null,"banner":null,"screenshots":[],"listed":false,"numComments":0,"numRatings":0,"avgRating":0,"numViews":76,"scoreCompleteness":50,"groupDesignations":null,"apiToken1ExpirationDate":-1,"apiToken2ExpirationDate":-1,"lastViewed":1790870400000},{"id":"7611d835cfba499a9ef64a172fcaa734","owner":"seerai","created":1658439555000,"modified":1779129938000,"guid":null,"name":null,"title":"ExxonMobil PNG LNG Monitoring","type":"StoryMap","typeKeywords":["arcgis-storymaps","smdraftresourceid:draft_1779129939760.json","smfirstpublisheddate:1658451945969","smpublisheddate:1779129939760","smstatuspublished","smversiondraft:26.17.1239","smversionpublished:26.17.1239","StoryMap","Web Application","Registered App","App Proxy"],"description":"<p>ExxonMobil, Papua New Guinea (PNG) LNG business unit consists of a 700-kilometer-long pipeline which runs from the Highlands to an LNG (liquefied natural gas) plant outside of Port Moresby. The pipeline has both an onshore and offshore section. This project concerns the onshore section of the pipeline which has a diameter between 32 and 34 inches and is buried one meter below ground with some short portions exposed above ground for maintenance purposes. The entire pipeline is composed of over 34,000 separate pipe sections [1].<\/p><p>ExxonMobil PNG LNG monitors this pipeline through a combination of internal and external systems in order to: 1) ensure the continued integrity of the pipeline through regular maintenance; and 2) identify any events/incidents occurring that require immediate attention.  <\/p>","tags":[""],"snippet":"Data Fusion to Monitor Pipeline Risk in Near Real Time","thumbnail":"thumbnail/thumbnail.jpeg","documentation":null,"extent":[],"categories":[],"spatialReference":null,"accessInformation":null,"classification":null,"licenseInfo":null,"culture":"en-us","properties":{"thumbnail":["url","https:/